        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you are sealing your own asphalt driveway and you are not sure which way to go, use a squeegee. It puts more sealer where it needs to be, it works with the sanded products sold in pails, it costs about thirty dollars, and it forgives a rough surface that a sprayer would struggle with. The trade is physical effort and a finish that looks hand-applied rather than machine-applied.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Reach for a sprayer when the asphalt is smooth and tight, when the area is large enough that squeegeeing becomes a genuine endurance event, and when you have the right equipment and are willing to mask properly. Then broom the material in behind the spray pass, the way commercial crews do, and apply a second coat.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whichever method you choose, the variables that actually determine how long the job lasts are the ones people skip: a genuinely clean surface, every crack filled and cured first, two thin coats rather than one thick one, and a settled weather window above 50°F with no rain for 24 to 48 hours. Get those right and the applicator in your hands matters far less than the internet suggests.
          </p>
        </div>

